# Text meant to be pasted carries its own line breaks

Unscoped on purpose: the client message, the form answer or the listing that
the owner will paste elsewhere can be the deliverable of any session, and the
damage is done at the moment the block is written.

`CLAUDE.md` says such text goes in a fenced block, one paragraph per unbroken
line. What it omits is the cost: WhatsApp, Slack, mail and web forms reflow
text themselves, so a paragraph hard-wrapped at 80 columns arrives ragged and
has to be rejoined by hand — the exact work the block existed to save. Leading
spaces are worse: the destination renders the message as a code listing.

So no line of the block is indented, a paragraph is one line however long, and
blank lines are the only breaks. Never run a formatter over such a block. The
terminal soft-wraps it for reading; the clipboard still holds one line.
